/* Hinglish: part4, plain-language rewrite (v5). Keyed on the exact English line.
   Written to be read aloud: spoken Hinglish, English for every word people say in English at work. */
Object.assign(window.HING = window.HING || {}, {

  /* ---- ch185: Build or buy: comparing a vendor with your own build ---- */
  'Build or buy: comparing a vendor with your own build':
    'Build or buy: vendor ki apne build se comparison',
  'Build-or-buy decisions often turn on price and demo quality, which matter less than they seem. You will test a vendor with your own answer key, cost both options over two years, and write down in advance what would change your decision.':
    'Build ya buy ka decision aksar price aur demo quality par aa tikta hai, lekin yeh dono utne zaroori nahi hote jitne lagte hain. Aap vendor ko apne answer key se test karenge, dono options ki do saal ki cost nikaalenge, aur pehle se likh lenge ki kaun si baat aapka decision badal degi.',
  'A spec describes a measured range':
    'Ek spec measured range batata hai',
  'So you can define “good enough” before you compare options.':
    'Isse aap options compare karne se pehle tay kar sakte hain ki “kaafi achha” kya hota hai.',
  'You can measure retrieval yourself':
    'Aap retrieval khud measure kar sakte hain',
  'So you can test a vendor instead of trusting a demo.':
    'Isse aap demo par bharosa karne ki jagah vendor ko test kar sakte hain.',
  'Run the same evaluation on a vendor and on your own build, and compare like with like.':
    'Vendor aur apne build, dono par wahi evaluation chalana, aur barabar ki cheezon ko compare karna.',
  'Name what you are really buying, which is usually not the model.':
    'Bata paana ki aap asal mein kya kharid rahe hain, aur woh aksar model nahi hota.',
  'Write down what would make you change the decision later, before you make it.':
    'Decision lene se pehle hi likh paana ki aage chal kar kaun si baat aapka decision badal degi.',
  'Before you start':
    'Shuru karne se pehle',
  'No new setup. Bring the acceptance criteria from Chapter 18 and the answer key from Chapter 6.':
    'Koi naya setup nahi. Chapter 18 ke acceptance criteria aur Chapter 6 ka answer key saath rakhiye.',
  'In the last chapter you wrote a full requirements document and planned for the day a model is retired. Now imagine a colleague walks in and says, “There’s a product that already does this. Why are we building it?”':
    'Pichhle chapter mein aapne ek poora requirements document likha aur us din ki taiyaari ki jab ek model retire hota hai. Ab maan lijiye ek colleague aakar kehta hai, “Ek product pehle se yahi karta hai. Hum ise khud kyun bana rahe hain?”',
  'That question will come up sooner or later. The discussion usually turns to price and to how good the demo looked. Neither one decides the matter.':
    'Yeh sawaal kabhi na kabhi zaroor aayega. Baat aksar price par aur is par aa jaati hai ki demo kitna achha laga. In dono mein se koi bhi faisla nahi karta.',
  'You are rarely buying the model, because most vendors use the same models. You are buying the connectors, the permissions, the audit trail, the support contract, and someone else handling incidents.':
    'Aap shayad hi kabhi model kharidte hain, kyunki zyadatar vendors wahi models use karte hain. Aap kharidte hain connectors, permissions, audit trail, support contract, aur yeh suvidha ki incidents koi aur sambhaale.',
  'Test the vendor with your answer key':
    'Vendor ko apne answer key se test kijiye',
  'A demo is built to look good. To see what a vendor can really do, you need a test the vendor didn’t design. Luckily, you already have one.':
    'Demo isliye banaya jaata hai ki achha dikhe. Vendor asal mein kya kar sakta hai, yeh dekhne ke liye aapko aisa test chahiye jo vendor ne design nahi kiya. Achhi baat yeh hai ki aapke paas pehle se hai.',
  'Run your answer key against the vendor':
    'Apna answer key vendor par chalaiye',
  'Ask for a trial. Run your ten real questions through it and grade the answers against your key. Use your questions, not the vendor’s demo questions.':
    'Trial maangiye. Apne dus asli sawaal usmein chalaiye aur jawaabon ko apne key ke against grade kijiye. Apne sawaal use kijiye, vendor ke demo ke sawaal nahi.',
  'The result is usually worse than the demo suggested and better than you feared. The unanswerable question and the second-language question usually show the biggest gap, because vendors rarely optimise for them.':
    'Result aksar demo se bura aur aapke darr se achha nikalta hai. Jis sawaal ka jawaab hai hi nahi, aur doosri language wala sawaal, inmein aksar sabse bada gap dikhta hai, kyunki vendors inke liye shayad hi optimise karte hain.',
  'Tip':
    'Tip',
  'You are the only person in the negotiation who can run this test, because you have the answer key.':
    'Negotiation mein sirf aap hi yeh test chala sakte hain, kyunki answer key aapke paas hai.',
  'The questions that decide it':
    'Woh sawaal jo decide karte hain',
  'Once you’ve tested the vendor, four questions help you decide. The table gives each question and the reason it matters.':
    'Vendor ko test karne ke baad, chaar sawaal decide karne mein madad karte hain. Table mein har sawaal hai aur yeh ki woh kyun zaroori hai.',
  'Question':
    'Sawaal',
  'Why it matters':
    'Yeh kyun zaroori hai',
  'Is this a core capability or a supporting one?':
    'Yeh core capability hai ya supporting?',
  'If your product exists to do this, you need to own it. If it is supporting plumbing, buying is usually right.':
    'Agar aapka product yahi karne ke liye bana hai, toh ise aapko apna rakhna hoga. Agar yeh sirf supporting plumbing hai, toh kharidna aksar sahi hota hai.',
  'How often will it need to change?':
    'Ise kitni baar badalna padega?',
  'Something you tune weekly against your own data is hard to buy. Something stable is expensive to build.':
    'Jo cheez aap har hafte apne data par tune karte hain, use kharidna mushkil hai. Jo cheez stable hai, use banana mehnga hai.',
  'Who handles incidents at 2am?':
    'Raat 2 baje incidents kaun sambhaalta hai?',
  'A real cost that is often left out, and often larger than the licence.':
    'Ek asli cost jo aksar chhoot jaati hai, aur aksar licence se bhi badi hoti hai.',
  'What happens if the vendor is acquired or shuts down?':
    'Agar vendor ko koi aur kharid le ya woh band ho jaaye toh kya hoga?',
  'Ask what you would need in order to leave, and how long it would take. If nobody can answer, treat that as a risk.':
    'Poochhiye ki wahan se nikalne ke liye aapko kya chahiye hoga, aur ismein kitna time lagega. Agar koi jawaab nahi de paata, toh ise ek risk maniye.',
  'Those questions are about the shape of the decision. Next, put numbers on it.':
    'Yeh sawaal decision ki shape ke baare mein hain. Ab ismein numbers daaliye.',
  'Cost both options over two years':
    'Dono options ki do saal ki cost nikaaliye',
  'Write the two-year cost of each option. For building, include the Chapter 15 costs plus what people usually leave out: evaluation, the person maintaining it, and rerunning the migration tests every time the underlying model changes.':
    'Har option ki do saal ki cost likhiye. Build ke liye Chapter 15 ki costs jodiye, aur woh bhi jo log aksar chhod dete hain: evaluation, ise maintain karne wala insaan, aur har baar jab neeche ka model badle tab migration tests dobara chalana.',
  'The build cost is usually two to four times the first estimate. The buy option often has a price increase at the next tier that nobody has read. Find both before you decide.':
    'Build ki cost aksar pehle ke andaaze se do se chaar guna zyada hoti hai. Buy wale option mein aksar agle tier par price badhta hai jise kisi ne padha nahi hota. Decide karne se pehle dono dhoondhiye.',
  'Then add the time until each option reaches a user. A build that is cheaper over two years but arrives six months later can still be the wrong choice.':
    'Phir yeh time jodiye ki har option kitne time mein user tak pahunchega. Ek build jo do saal mein sasta hai lekin chhe mahine der se aata hai, woh bhi galat choice ho sakta hai.',
  'Decide what would change your mind':
    'Tay kijiye ki kya aapka mann badal dega',
  'Decisions like this are rarely final. So before you make yours, decide what new facts would make you reverse it.':
    'Aise decisions shayad hi aakhri hote hain. Isliye apna decision lene se pehle tay kijiye ki kaun se naye facts aapko use palatne par majboor karenge.',
  'Write down what would make you change your mind, in both directions. What evidence would turn a build recommendation into a buy, and what would turn a buy into a build?':
    'Likhiye ki kaun si baat aapka mann badal degi, dono taraf ke liye. Kaun sa evidence build ki recommendation ko buy mein badal dega, aur kaun sa buy ko build mein?',
  'I would switch to buying if … . I would switch to building if … .':
    'Main buy karne par switch karunga agar … . Main build karne par switch karunga agar … .',
  'Writing this before deciding keeps the decision open to evidence. Common reasons to switch to buying: the maintenance falls on one person, or the capability turns out to be a commodity. Common reasons to switch to building: the vendor’s roadmap moves away from your needs, the price rises at the next tier, or the “plumbing” turns out to be why customers choose you. Put a date on when you will review it.':
    'Decision se pehle yeh likhne se decision evidence ke liye khula rehta hai. Buy par switch karne ki aam wajahein: maintenance ek hi insaan par aa jaaye, ya yeh capability commodity nikle. Build par switch karne ki aam wajahein: vendor ki roadmap aapki zaroorat se door chali jaaye, agle tier par price badhe, ya “plumbing” hi woh wajah nikle jisse customers aapko chunte hain. Review ki ek date bhi likh dijiye.',
  'A common good answer is a mix: buy the plumbing and build the part that makes your product different. For example, use a vendor’s connectors and permissions, but own your retrieval and your evaluation.':
    'Ek aam achha jawaab mix hota hai: plumbing kharid lijiye aur woh hissa banaiye jo aapke product ko alag banata hai. Jaise, vendor ke connectors aur permissions use kijiye, lekin retrieval aur evaluation apne paas rakhiye.',
  'To sum up: test the vendor on your own answer key, cost both options over two years, and write down what would change your mind. Next, you’ll look at a different choice, which is whether to fine-tune a model or change something else.':
    'Toh matlab: vendor ko apne answer key par test kijiye, dono options ki do saal ki cost nikaaliye, aur likhiye ki kya aapka mann badal dega. Agle chapter mein aap ek alag choice dekhenge: model ko fine-tune karein ya kuch aur badlein.',
  'Recommend whether to build each part yourself or buy it':
    'Tay kijiye ki har hissa khud banana hai ya kharidna hai',
  'For each part of the tool there is usually a ready-made option to buy or borrow, and your own build to compare it with. In this chapter you learned to run the same test on both. You also learned to name what you are really buying, which is often not the model, and to write down what would change your mind before you decide. Now you do that for two parts of Bharat Privacy Guard.':
    'Tool ke har hisse ke liye aksar kharidne ya udhaar lene ka ek ready-made option hota hai, aur compare karne ke liye aapka apna build. Is chapter mein aapne dono par wahi test chalana seekha. Aapne yeh bhi seekha ki aap asal mein kya kharid rahe hain, jo aksar model nahi hota, aur decision se pehle yeh likhna ki kya baat aapka mann badal degi. Ab aap Bharat Privacy Guard ke do hisson ke liye yeh kijiye.',
  'Mostly written. Use your answer key to test each option, in a notebook if the option has code, and write the recommendation in the notes box below.':
    'Zyadatar likhna hai. Har option ko test karne ke liye apna answer key use kijiye, notebook mein agar option mein code ho, aur recommendation neeche notes box mein likhiye.',
  'Choose two parts of the tool: the pattern checker and the name-and-place finder. For each part, write in one line what it must do and whether it is the heart of your product or a supporting piece.':
    'Tool ke do hisse chuniye: pattern checker aur name-and-place finder. Har hisse ke liye ek line mein likhiye ki use kya karna hai, aur woh aapke product ka dil hai ya ek supporting piece.',
  'For the pattern checker, compare your own rules with an existing library such as Presidio’s recognisers. For the name-and-place finder, compare a ready-made multilingual model with one you would train or adapt yourself. Run each option on your answer key and put the results in one table: quality on English, Hindi and Hinglish, speed, cost and effort.':
    'Pattern checker ke liye apne rules ko kisi existing library, jaise Presidio ke recognisers, se compare kijiye. Name-and-place finder ke liye ek ready-made multilingual model ko us model se compare kijiye jo aap khud train ya adapt karenge. Har option ko apne answer key par chalaiye aur results ek table mein rakhiye: English, Hindi aur Hinglish par quality, speed, cost aur effort.',
  'Cost each option over two years, including testing, upkeep and the time to deal with incidents, not only the first build.':
    'Har option ki do saal ki cost nikaaliye, testing, upkeep aur incidents sambhaalne ka time bhi jodkar, sirf pehla build nahi.',
  'For each bought option, write what you would need in order to leave it later, and how long that would take.':
    'Har kharide hue option ke liye likhiye ki baad mein use chhodne ke liye aapko kya chahiye hoga, aur ismein kitna time lagega.',
  'Write what you are really buying. It is often updates, support and someone else carrying the responsibility, not the model itself.':
    'Likhiye ki aap asal mein kya kharid rahe hain. Yeh aksar updates, support aur kisi aur ka zimmedari uthana hota hai, model khud nahi.',
  'Write your recommendation for each part in two sentences. Add the two conditions that would reverse it and the date you will review it. Write these before you decide, not afterwards.':
    'Har hisse ke liye do sentences mein apni recommendation likhiye. Woh do conditions jodiye jo use palat degi, aur review ki date. Yeh decision se pehle likhiye, baad mein nahi.',
  'A table in which the options for each part were tested on the same answer key.':
    'Ek table jisme har hisse ke options ko ek hi answer key par test kiya gaya.',
  'A two-year cost for each option that includes upkeep and testing.':
    'Har option ki do saal ki cost, jisme upkeep aur testing shaamil hai.',
  'A note on what it would take to leave each bought option.':
    'Ek note ki har kharide hue option ko chhodne ke liye kya karna padega.',
  'Two recommendations, each with its reversing conditions and a review date.':
    'Do recommendations, har ek ke saath use palatne wali conditions aur ek review date.',

  /* ---- ch19: Fine-tuning or not: choosing what to change ---- */
  'Fine-tuning or not: choosing what to change':
    'Fine-tuning karein ya nahi: kya badalna hai, yeh kaise chunein',
  'When a model is not good enough, you can change the instructions, the evidence, the training or the model. In this chapter you’ll sort real failures into four kinds, match each kind to its fix, and work out what a fine-tuning proposal would really cost.':
    'Jab model kaafi achha na ho, toh aap instructions, evidence, training ya model, kuch bhi badal sakte hain. Is chapter mein aap asli failures ko chaar kinds mein baantenge, har kind ko uske fix se jodenge, aur dekhenge ki fine-tuning ka proposal asal mein kitne mein padega.',
  'A list of your own failures, with counts':
    'Aapke apne failures ki list, counts ke saath',
  'You can’t choose a fix until you know what is going wrong, and how often.':
    'Jab tak aapko pata nahi ki kya galat ho raha hai aur kitni baar, aap fix nahi chun sakte.',
  'A test set you trust':
    'Ek test set jis par aap bharosa kar sakein',
  'Without one, you can’t see what any change in this chapter actually did.':
    'Iske bina aap dekh hi nahi sakte ki is chapter ke kisi bhi badlaav ka asar kya hua.',
  'Name the four things you can change, and what each one actually fixes.':
    'Bata paana ki aap chaar cheezein kaun si badal sakte hain, aur har ek asal mein kya theek karti hai.',
  'Explain why fine-tuning is the wrong tool for “it does not know our new policy”.':
    'Samjha paana ki “yeh hamari nayi policy nahi jaanta” wali problem ke liye fine-tuning galat tool kyun hai.',
  'Say what must exist before the fine-tuning question can be answered.':
    'Bata paana ki fine-tuning ke sawaal ka jawaab dene se pehle kya hona zaroori hai.',
  'You don’t need a notebook for this chapter. Open your failure taxonomy from Chapter 14.5, or collect ten real outputs from an AI feature you use every day.':
    'Is chapter ke liye notebook nahi chahiye. Chapter 14.5 ka apna failure taxonomy kholiye, ya kisi AI feature ke, jo aap roz use karte hain, das asli outputs jama kijiye.',
  'In the last chapter you compared buying from a vendor with building your own. Now a different question comes up, and sooner or later someone will ask it in a roadmap review: <em>should we fine-tune?</em>':
    'Pichhle chapter mein aapne vendor se kharidne aur khud banane ka comparison dekha. Ab ek alag sawaal aata hai, aur kabhi na kabhi koi roadmap review mein yeh poochega zaroor: <em>should we fine-tune?</em>',
  'The question usually arrives before anyone has said what is actually wrong. Saying yes commits the team to a quarter of work. Saying no can sound unambitious. So the right answer starts with a diagnosis, and this chapter shows you how to make one.':
    'Yeh sawaal aksar tab aata hai jab kisi ne abhi tak bataya hi nahi ki asal mein galat kya hai. Haan kehne par team ek quarter ke kaam mein bandh jaati hai. Naa kehna unambitious lag sakta hai. Isliye sahi jawaab diagnosis se shuru hota hai, aur yeh chapter aapko dikhata hai ki woh diagnosis kaise karte hain.',
  'Four things you can change':
    'Chaar cheezein jo aap badal sakte hain',
  'Suppose an AI feature isn’t good enough. What can you actually change? There are four things. You can change <strong>the instructions</strong>, or <strong>the evidence you give the model</strong>. You can change <strong>the model’s training on your own examples</strong>, which is called fine-tuning. Or you can change <strong>which model you use</strong>.':
    'Maan lijiye ek AI feature kaafi achha nahi hai. Aap asal mein kya badal sakte hain? Chaar cheezein hain. Aap <strong>instructions</strong> badal sakte hain, ya <strong>woh evidence jo aap model ko dete hain</strong>. Aap <strong>apne examples par model ki training</strong> badal sakte hain, jise fine-tuning kehte hain. Ya phir aap <strong>kaunsa model use karna hai</strong>, yeh badal sakte hain.',
  'Try them in that order. Let’s see why, with a failure you may well meet.':
    'Inhe isi order mein try kijiye. Ek aisi failure se dekhte hain kyun, jo aapke saamne aa bhi sakti hai.',
  'Which one, and why':
    'Kaunsa, aur kyun',
  'Predict: your assistant confidently says the refund window is 30 days. The correct figure, published last month, is 45. Which of the four changes fixes it, and which will a meeting room usually suggest?':
    'Andaaza lagaiye: aapka assistant poore confidence se kehta hai ki refund window 30 din ki hai. Sahi figure, jo pichhle mahine publish hui, 45 hai. Chaaron mein se kaunsa badlaav ise theek karta hai, aur meeting room mein aam taur par kaunsa suggest hota hai?',
  'Better evidence. The system does not have the current policy, so it produces a plausible number from its training, as Chapter 2 described. A meeting will often suggest fine-tuning, because “teach the model our policies” sounds right. But a fine-tuned model needs retraining every time the number changes, and it still cannot show the user where 45 came from.':
    'Better evidence. System ke paas current policy hai hi nahi, isliye woh apni training se ek plausible number bana deta hai, jaisa Chapter 2 mein bataya gaya tha. Meeting mein aksar fine-tuning suggest hoti hai, kyunki “model ko hamari policies sikhaa do” sunne mein sahi lagta hai. Lekin fine-tuned model ko har baar retrain karna padta hai jab number badle, aur woh phir bhi user ko yeh nahi dikha sakta ki 45 kahan se aaya.',
  'This is one of the most common misdiagnoses in the field. It is expensive because the wrong answer sounds so reasonable.':
    'Yeh field ki sabse common galat diagnosis mein se ek hai. Yeh mehngi isliye padti hai kyunki galat jawaab sunne mein bahut reasonable lagta hai.',
  'The table below puts all four changes side by side. As you read it, look at the last two columns. They show what each change cannot fix, and how hard it is to try and to undo.':
    'Neeche wali table chaaron badlaavon ko saath-saath rakhti hai. Padhte waqt aakhri do columns dekhiye. Woh batate hain ki har badlaav kya theek nahi kar sakta, aur use try karna aur wapas lena kitna mushkil hai.',
  'What you change':
    'Aap kya badalte hain',
  'What it really fixes':
    'Woh asal mein kya theek karta hai',
  'What it cannot fix':
    'Woh kya theek nahi kar sakta',
  'Cost to try, and to undo':
    'Try karne aur wapas lene ki cost',
  'Instructions':
    'Instructions',
  'Format, tone, when to refuse, and how the task is framed':
    'Format, tone, kab refuse karna hai, aur task ko kaise frame kiya gaya hai',
  'Missing knowledge, and real limits in reasoning':
    'Missing knowledge, aur reasoning ki asli limits',
  'Minutes to try, and undone instantly':
    'Try karne mein minutes lagte hain, aur turant wapas ho jaata hai',
  'Evidence':
    'Evidence',
  'Missing, changing or private knowledge, and citations':
    'Missing, badalta hua ya private knowledge, aur citations',
  'Problems with behaviour and format':
    'Behaviour aur format ki problems',
  'Days to try, and undone by rebuilding the index':
    'Try karne mein din lagte hain, aur index dobara banane se wapas hota hai',
  'Training on examples':
    'Examples par training',
  'A consistent shape and register, cheaply, and a small model doing one job well':
    'Ek consistent shape aur register, sasta mein, aur ek chhota model jo ek kaam achhe se kare',
  'Facts that change, and bad retrieval':
    'Woh facts jo badalte rehte hain, aur kharab retrieval',
  'Weeks to try, plus upkeep forever. It is not undone, only retrained':
    'Try karne mein hafte lagte hain, aur upar se hamesha upkeep. Yeh wapas nahi hota, sirf dobara train hota hai',
  'A different model':
    'Alag model',
  'Reasoning depth, speed, and cost per query':
    'Reasoning ki gehraai, speed, aur har query ki cost',
  'Bad evidence, because a better model just reasons better over the wrong document':
    'Kharab evidence, kyunki behtar model galat document par bhi bas behtar reasoning kar leta hai',
  'Hours to try, but it reopens every measurement you have':
    'Try karne mein ghante lagte hain, lekin aapki har measurement dobara kholni padti hai',
  'Diagnose the kind of failure':
    'Failure ki kind pehchaaniye',
  'Now you can see why the order matters. Before you ask “which fix?”, ask “what kind of failure is this?”. There are four kinds, and each one points to its own fix.':
    'Ab aap dekh sakte hain ki order kyun matter karta hai. “Kaunsa fix?” poochne se pehle poochiye, “yeh kaisi failure hai?”. Chaar kinds hain, aur har ek ka apna fix hai.',
  '<strong>It does not know.</strong> The answer is missing, out of date or invented. The fix is better evidence, and versions for your documents (Chapter 12).':
    '<strong>Use pata nahi hai.</strong> Jawaab missing hai, purana hai ya banaya hua hai. Fix hai better evidence, aur apne documents ke versions (Chapter 12).',
  '<strong>It knows, but behaves wrongly.</strong> The content is right, but the format, tone or rule-following is wrong. Improve the instructions first, then require a format (Chapter 8). Fine-tune only if those stop helping.':
    '<strong>Use pata hai, lekin woh galat behave karta hai.</strong> Content sahi hai, lekin format, tone ya rules follow karna galat hai. Pehle instructions behtar kijiye, phir format ko zaroori banaiye (Chapter 8). Fine-tune tabhi kijiye jab yeh dono madad karna band kar dein.',
  '<strong>It cannot work it out.</strong> The task needs several steps of logic, calculations that depend on each other, or real ambiguity. The fix is to use reasoning or a stronger model (Chapter 11).':
    '<strong>Woh ise solve nahi kar paata.</strong> Task mein kai steps ka logic hai, ek doosre par depend karte calculations hain, ya asli ambiguity hai. Fix hai reasoning ya ek stronger model use karna (Chapter 11).',
  '<strong>It is right but too expensive.</strong> It is too slow or too costly at your volume. The fix is a smaller model, or a cheap-first cascade, which tries the cheap model before the costly one (Chapter 15).':
    '<strong>Jawaab sahi hai, lekin bahut mehnga hai.</strong> Aapke volume par woh bahut slow ya bahut costly hai. Fix hai ek chhota model, ya cheap-first cascade, jo mehnge model se pehle sasta model try karta hai (Chapter 15).',
  'The best way to learn this is to do it on real failures. So take your list and sort it.':
    'Yeh seekhne ka sabse achha tareeka hai ise asli failures par karna. Toh apni list lijiye aur use baant dijiye.',
  'Sort real failures into the four kinds':
    'Asli failures ko chaar kinds mein baantiye',
  'Next to each failure, write one letter: <strong>K</strong> (does not know), <strong>B</strong> (behaves wrongly), <strong>R</strong> (cannot work it out) or <strong>E</strong> (too expensive). Choose a single letter even when it is hard. The hard ones teach you the most.':
    'Har failure ke saath ek letter likhiye: <strong>K</strong> (pata nahi), <strong>B</strong> (galat behave karta hai), <strong>R</strong> (solve nahi kar paata) ya <strong>E</strong> (bahut mehnga). Mushkil lage tab bhi ek hi letter chuniye. Mushkil wale cases aapko sabse zyada sikhaate hain.',
  'The result is usually uneven. Most real failure lists are dominated by K and B. That is why better retrieval and better prompting produce most of the improvement in most products, and why fine-tuning proposals often fail to deliver.':
    'Result aksar ek taraf jhuka hota hai. Zyadatar asli failure lists mein K aur B hi bahut zyada hote hain. Isliye zyadatar products mein better retrieval aur better prompting se hi sabse zyada improvement aata hai, aur isliye fine-tuning ke proposals aksar wada poora nahi kar paate.',
  'Fine-tuning teaches behaviour, not facts. If the complaint is “it does not know our policy”, fine-tuning is the wrong tool.':
    'Fine-tuning behaviour sikhaati hai, facts nahi. Agar shikayat yeh hai ki “yeh hamari policy nahi jaanta”, toh fine-tuning galat tool hai.',
  'Notice what this diagnosis needs: a named failure with a count behind it. That is your Chapter 14.5 list. Without it, the discussion turns into a contest of opinions, and the most senior opinion usually wins.':
    'Dekhiye is diagnosis ko kya chahiye: ek naam wali failure, jiske peeche ek count ho. Woh aapki Chapter 14.5 ki list hai. Uske bina baat opinions ki ladaai ban jaati hai, aur aam taur par sabse senior opinion jeet jaata hai.',
  'Diagnose a real failure you have seen in an AI feature, yours or one you use. Write what the user saw, which of the four kinds it is, and the fix. Then write the one measurement that would show you chose correctly.':
    'AI feature mein, aapke ya jo aap use karte hain, ek asli failure ko diagnose kijiye. Likhiye ki user ne kya dekha, woh chaar kinds mein se kaunsi hai, aur fix kya hai. Phir woh ek measurement likhiye jo dikhaye ki aapne sahi chuna.',
  'the user saw … kind of failure … the fix … I would know I was right if …':
    'user ne dekha … failure ki kind … fix … mujhe pata chalega ki main sahi tha agar …',
  'Classifying is the hard part, because failures often look alike. “It gave a confident wrong figure” could be any of the four until you check what was retrieved. If the right passage was retrieved and misread, it is behaviour or reasoning. If the wrong passage was retrieved, only better retrieval helps. If the right passage is not in the documents at all, the fix comes before anything else in this course. The measurement matters too. “The right passage is retrieved 9 times in 10 instead of 6” can be checked, but “it feels better” cannot.':
    'Classify karna mushkil hissa hai, kyunki failures aksar ek jaisi dikhti hain. “Usne confident galat figure di” chaaron mein se kuch bhi ho sakta hai, jab tak aap check na karein ki kya retrieve hua tha. Agar sahi passage retrieve hua tha aur galat padha gaya, toh yeh behaviour ya reasoning hai. Agar galat passage retrieve hua, toh sirf better retrieval madad karegi. Agar sahi passage documents mein hai hi nahi, toh fix is course ki kisi bhi aur cheez se pehle aata hai. Measurement bhi matter karti hai. “Sahi passage 6 ki jagah 10 mein se 9 baar retrieve hota hai” check kiya ja sakta hai, lekin “better lag raha hai” nahi.',
  'What fine-tuning really costs':
    'Fine-tuning asal mein kitne mein padti hai',
  'None of this means fine-tuning is a bad idea. It does give real benefits. First, it gives <strong>consistency</strong>: the same structure and tone across thousands of outputs, more reliably than instructions can.':
    'Iska matlab yeh nahi ki fine-tuning buri idea hai. Isse asli fayde milte hain. Pehla, <strong>consistency</strong>: hazaaron outputs mein ek jaisa structure aur tone, jo instructions se zyada bharosemand tareeke se aata hai.',
  'Second, it lets a <strong>small model do a narrow job</strong> about as well as a large one, which saves a lot of money. And third, it teaches conventions that are hard to describe but easy to show, such as house style.':
    'Doosra, ek <strong>chhota model ek narrow kaam</strong> lagbhag utne hi achhe se kar sakta hai jitna ek bada model, aur isse bahut paisa bachta hai. Aur teesra, yeh woh conventions sikhaati hai jinhe describe karna mushkil hai lekin dikhana aasaan hai, jaise house style.',
  'The catch is that proposals usually leave out the costs. Someone must create the training examples and <em>keep them up to date</em>. You need a test set in advance, or you can’t tell whether it helped. You must retrain every time the base model is retired, as in Chapter 18. And your improvement now lives inside one provider’s system, instead of in your prompt and your index.':
    'Dikkat yeh hai ki proposals aksar costs chhod dete hain. Kisi ko training examples banane padte hain aur unhe <em>up to date rakhna</em> padta hai. Aapko pehle se ek test set chahiye, warna aap bata nahi sakte ki isse madad mili ya nahi. Har baar jab base model retire hota hai, aapko dobara retrain karna padta hai, jaise Chapter 18 mein tha. Aur aapka improvement ab ek provider ke system ke andar rehta hai, aapke prompt aur index mein nahi.',
  'Cost a fine-tuning proposal':
    'Fine-tuning proposal ki cost nikaaliye',
  'Take the most likely fine-tuning candidate from your list and write down the full cost, not just the training bill. How many labelled examples will it need? Who writes them? Who maintains them when the product changes? What test proves it worked? And what happens to all of it when the base model is retired in eighteen months?':
    'Apni list se sabse likely fine-tuning candidate lijiye aur poori cost likhiye, sirf training ka bill nahi. Kitne labelled examples chahiye honge? Unhe kaun likhega? Product badalne par unhe kaun maintain karega? Kaunsa test saabit karega ki yeh kaam kiya? Aur jab atharah mahine mein base model retire hoga, toh in sab ka kya hoga?',
  'The total is several times the number on the vendor’s pricing page. You also end up with a list of owners. A fine-tuning proposal with no named owner for the training data will slowly decay.':
    'Total vendor ki pricing page ke number se kai guna zyada hota hai. Saath mein aapko owners ki ek list bhi mil jaati hai. Jis fine-tuning proposal mein training data ka koi named owner nahi hai, woh dheere-dheere kharab ho jaayega.',
  'Try a smaller model':
    'Chhota model try kijiye',
  'There is one more option, and teams test it least, even though it often has the biggest effect: <strong>going smaller</strong>. A cheaper, faster model can decide the economics of a high-volume feature. Sometimes it is even trained to imitate a larger model on your task.':
    'Ek aur option hai, jise teams sabse kam test karti hain, jabki aksar uska asar sabse bada hota hai: <strong>chhota model lena</strong>. Ek sasta, tez model high-volume feature ki economics tay kar sakta hai. Kabhi-kabhi use aapke task par bade model ki nakal karne ke liye train bhi kiya jaata hai.',
  'Small models do well on tasks that are narrow, repetitive, high in volume and sensitive to time. Let’s estimate what that could save you.':
    'Chhote models un tasks par achhe chalte hain jo narrow hon, baar-baar hone wale hon, jinka volume zyada ho aur jahan time matter karta ho. Chaliye andaaza lagate hain ki isse kitna bach sakta hai.',
  'Estimate the saving from a smaller model':
    'Chhote model se hone wali bachat ka andaaza lagaiye',
  'Pick the narrowest, highest-volume task in your product, such as routing, classification, extraction or a short summary. Estimate its share of your total query volume. Then use the cost model below to work out what it would cost on a model one or two tiers cheaper.':
    'Apne product ka sabse narrow, sabse high-volume task chuniye, jaise routing, classification, extraction ya ek chhota summary. Andaaza lagaiye ki total query volume mein uska hissa kitna hai. Phir neeche wale cost model se nikaaliye ki ek ya do tier sasta model use karne par uski cost kitni hogi.',
  'This is often the largest single saving available, and it sits in the least exciting part of the product. Most teams never test it.':
    'Aksar yeh sabse badi akeli bachat hoti hai, aur woh product ke sabse boring hisse mein chhupi hoti hai. Zyadatar teams ise kabhi test nahi karti.',
  'Work in this order: instructions, then evidence, then fine-tuning, then a different model. Measure after each one. The first two can be undone in hours, so ruling them out is cheap. Fine-tuning commits you to maintenance for as long as the feature exists.':
    'Is order mein kaam kijiye: instructions, phir evidence, phir fine-tuning, phir alag model. Har ek ke baad measure kijiye. Pehle do cheezein ghanton mein wapas ho sakti hain, isliye unhe rule out karna sasta hai. Fine-tuning aapko tab tak maintenance mein bandh kar deti hai jab tak feature chalta hai.',
  'Summary':
    'Summary',
  'So here is the whole chapter in a few lines. Name the failure before you pick a fix. Most failures are about missing knowledge or wrong behaviour, and those are fixed more cheaply than by training. Fine-tuning has real uses, but it comes with a cost that lasts as long as the feature does.':
    'Toh poora chapter chhote mein yeh hai. Fix chunne se pehle failure ka naam lijiye. Zyadatar failures missing knowledge ya galat behaviour ki hoti hain, aur unhe training se zyada sasta tareeke se theek kiya ja sakta hai. Fine-tuning ke asli use hain, lekin uski cost utni der chalti hai jitni der feature chalta hai.',
  'You can’t see the effect of any of the four changes without a test set. A team with an answer key can settle “should we fine-tune?” in a week. A team without one can’t settle it at all.':
    'Chaaron badlaavon mein se kisi ka bhi asar aap test set ke bina nahi dekh sakte. Jis team ke paas answer key hai, woh “should we fine-tune?” ka faisla ek hafte mein kar sakti hai. Jis team ke paas nahi hai, woh kabhi nahi kar sakti.',
  'Next, you’ll look at what the screen should do when the model gets an answer wrong, which at some rate it always will.':
    'Aage aap dekhenge ki jab model galat jawaab de, toh screen ko kya karna chahiye, kyunki kisi na kisi rate par woh hamesha dega.',
  'Answer a proposal to fine-tune a model, using evidence':
    'Evidence ke saath fine-tuning ke proposal ka jawaab dijiye',
  'Someone suggests that the name-and-place finder could be improved by training a small Indian-language model of your own. That is called fine-tuning, and it sounds like the serious option. In this chapter you learned the four things you can change in an AI system, what each one fixes, and why fine-tuning is the wrong tool for “it does not know our new policy”. Now you’ll decide, with evidence, whether the proposal is right.':
    'Koi suggest karta hai ki name-and-place finder ko behtar banane ke liye aap apna ek chhota Indian-language model train karein. Ise fine-tuning kehte hain, aur yeh sunne mein serious option lagta hai. Is chapter mein aapne seekha ki AI system mein aap kaun si chaar cheezein badal sakte hain, har ek kya theek karti hai, aur “yeh hamari nayi policy nahi jaanta” ke liye fine-tuning galat tool kyun hai. Ab aap evidence ke saath tay karenge ki proposal sahi hai ya nahi.',
  'This task is written, with no code. Use the failures you collected in earlier chapters and the numbers you already measured. Write your answer in the notes box below.':
    'Yeh task likhne ka hai, code nahi. Pichhle chapters mein jo failures aapne jama kiye aur jo numbers aapne measure kiye, unhe use kijiye. Apna jawaab neeche wale notes box mein likhiye.',
  'Collect twenty real failures from your tool. Take them from the list you made in Chapter 14.5.':
    'Apne tool ki bees asli failures jama kijiye. Unhe Chapter 14.5 mein banayi gayi list se lijiye.',
  'Sort the twenty into the four kinds from this chapter: the model did not know something, the instructions were unclear, the right information was not found, or the format was wrong.':
    'Un bees ko is chapter ki chaar kinds mein baantiye: model ko kuch pata nahi tha, instructions saaf nahi the, sahi information mili nahi, ya format galat tha.',
  'Count each kind. The counts often settle the question before any technical argument starts.':
    'Har kind ko gin lijiye. Count aksar kisi bhi technical bahas se pehle hi sawaal ka faisla kar dete hain.',
  'For the biggest group, name the cheapest change that would fix it, and explain why it is cheaper than fine-tuning.':
    'Sabse bade group ke liye woh sabse sasta badlaav batayiye jo use theek kar de, aur samjhaiye ki woh fine-tuning se sasta kyun hai.',
  'Work out the real cost of the fine-tuning proposal. Include preparing the data, training, testing, and doing all of it again every time the base model changes.':
    'Fine-tuning proposal ki asli cost nikaaliye. Isme data taiyaar karna, training, testing, aur har baar jab base model badle, yeh sab dobara karna shaamil kijiye.',
  'Write a one-page answer to whoever proposed it. Use the failure counts as your main argument, and say what evidence would change your mind.':
    'Jisne proposal diya, use ek page ka jawaab likhiye. Failure counts ko apni main dalil banaiye, aur batayiye ki kaunsa evidence aapka mann badal dega.',
  'Twenty real failures sorted into the four kinds, with a count for each.':
    'Bees asli failures chaar kinds mein baanti hui, har ek ke count ke saath.',
  'The cheapest alternative for the biggest group.':
    'Sabse bade group ke liye sabse sasta alternative.',
  'A cost for fine-tuning that includes the cost of repeating it.':
    'Fine-tuning ki ek cost jisme use dobara karne ki cost bhi shaamil ho.',
  'A one-page answer, including what evidence would change your recommendation.':
    'Ek page ka jawaab, jisme yeh bhi ho ki kaunsa evidence aapki recommendation badal dega.',

  /* ---- ch20: Designing for wrong answers: evidence, confidence and refusals ---- */
  'Designing for wrong answers: evidence, confidence and refusals':
    'Galat jawaab ke liye design: evidence, confidence aur refusals',
  'At 90% accuracy, one answer in ten is wrong. Whether the feature is usable depends on what the screen does about that one. In this chapter you’ll audit a real product, design four interface states, time a correction and design the refusal.':
    '90% accuracy par bhi 10 mein se ek jawaab galat hota hai. Feature kaam ka hai ya nahi, yeh is par depend karta hai ki us ek jawaab par screen kya karti hai. Is chapter mein aap ek asli product ka audit karenge, chaar interface states design karenge, ek correction ko time karenge aur refusal design karenge.',
  'A required quote makes checking cheap':
    'Zaroori quote se checking sasti ho jaati hai',
  'A quote is more useful than a confidence number.':
    'Ek quote confidence number se zyada kaam ka hota hai.',
  'Users notice the slow responses':
    'Users ko slow responses yaad rehte hain',
  'They notice the slowest ones, not the average response time.':
    'Unhe sabse slow wale yaad rehte hain, average response time nahi.',
  'A kill switch is not a rollback':
    'Kill switch rollback nahi hota',
  'Rolling back code does not undo behaviour that both versions share.':
    'Code rollback karne se woh behaviour wapas nahi hota jo dono versions mein ek jaisa hai.',
  'Explain how a feature that is wrong one time in ten can still be trusted.':
    'Samjha paana ki jo feature 10 mein se ek baar galat hota hai, us par bhi bharosa kaise kiya ja sakta hai.',
  'Say what to do with a confidence score, and why displaying it as a number is usually wrong.':
    'Bata paana ki confidence score ka kya karna chahiye, aur use number ki tarah dikhana aam taur par galat kyun hai.',
  'Explain what must exist underneath a kill switch for it to be usable.':
    'Samjha paana ki kill switch ke kaam karne ke liye uske neeche kya hona zaroori hai.',
  'You don’t need any code for this chapter. Pick an AI feature you use every day, and one feature you own or could own.':
    'Is chapter ke liye koi code nahi chahiye. Ek AI feature chuniye jo aap roz use karte hain, aur ek feature jo aapka hai ya ho sakta hai.',
  'In the last chapter you decided whether to change the model or something around it. Until now the course has been about making the model right more often. This chapter is about the rest of the time.':
    'Pichhle chapter mein aapne tay kiya ki model badalna hai ya uske aas-paas ki koi cheez. Ab tak course is baare mein tha ki model ko zyada baar sahi kaise banaya jaaye. Yeh chapter baaki samay ke baare mein hai.',
  'At 90% accuracy, one interaction in ten is wrong, and no amount of engineering removes that completely. So what the screen does in that case decides whether the feature is usable.':
    '90% accuracy par 10 mein se ek interaction galat hota hai, aur koi bhi engineering ise poori tarah nahi hata sakti. Isliye us waqt screen kya karti hai, yahi tay karta hai ki feature use karne laayak hai ya nahi.',
  'This is a design decision, and it belongs to the product owner. An engineer can tell you that a confidence score exists. The product owner decides what the user sees when that score is low.':
    'Yeh ek design decision hai, aur yeh product owner ka hai. Engineer aapko bata sakta hai ki confidence score maujood hai. Lekin score kam hone par user ko kya dikhega, yeh product owner tay karta hai.',
  'Do not show an uncertain answer in a confident interface. Many AI features feel untrustworthy because they show a wrong answer exactly the way they show a right one, not because they are less accurate.':
    'Ek uncertain jawaab ko confident interface mein mat dikhaiye. Bahut se AI features isliye bharose ke laayak nahi lagte ki woh galat jawaab ko bilkul waise hi dikhate hain jaise sahi jawaab ko, isliye nahi ki woh kam accurate hain.',
  'There are four things to get right. A feature that does all four can be wrong one time in ten and still be trusted. One that does none of them will be distrusted even at 95% accuracy. Let’s take them one by one.':
    'Chaar cheezein sahi karni hain. Jo feature chaaron karta hai, woh 10 mein se ek baar galat hokar bhi trusted rehta hai. Jo ek bhi nahi karta, use 95% accuracy par bhi shak ki nazar se dekha jaayega. Chaliye ek-ek karke dekhte hain.',
  '1. Show the evidence':
    '1. Evidence dikhaiye',
  'Start with a simple idea. If a user can check a claim in a few seconds, a wrong answer costs much less. A link that opens the actual source passage makes that possible. That is why Chapter 8 required the model to quote its source.':
    'Ek simple idea se shuru kijiye. Agar user kuch seconds mein kisi claim ko check kar sakta hai, toh galat jawaab ki cost bahut kam ho jaati hai. Ek link jo asli source passage kholta hai, yeh mumkin banata hai. Isliye Chapter 8 mein model se uska source quote karwana zaroori kiya gaya tha.',
  'A citation that names a document but does not open it is worse than nothing. It looks checkable, but the user can’t check it, and they won’t find that out until it matters.':
    'Aisi citation jo document ka naam batati hai lekin use kholti nahi, woh kuch na hone se bhi buri hai. Woh check karne laayak dikhti hai, lekin user check nahi kar sakta, aur use yeh tab pata chalta hai jab bahut der ho chuki hoti hai.',
  'Now try this on a product you already use.':
    'Ab yeh kisi aise product par try kijiye jo aap pehle se use karte hain.',
  'Audit an AI feature you use every day':
    'Roz use hone wale AI feature ka audit kijiye',
  'Pick one, such as a search assistant, an email drafter or a coding assistant. Ask it something it will get wrong, at the edge of what it knows.':
    'Ek chuniye, jaise search assistant, email drafter ya coding assistant. Usse aisi cheez poochiye jo woh galat karega, jahan uski knowledge ki limit khatam hoti hai.',
  'Then answer four questions in writing. Could you check the claim, and how many seconds did it take? Did the interface show the wrong answer any differently from a right one? What could you do about it? And what happened to your correction?':
    'Phir chaar sawaalon ke jawaab likhiye. Kya aap claim check kar paaye, aur kitne seconds lage? Kya interface ne galat jawaab ko sahi se alag tareeke se dikhaya? Aap uske baare mein kya kar sakte the? Aur aapke correction ka kya hua?',
  'Most well-known products fail at least two of the four. Note which ones. Your users will hold your feature to the same standard.':
    'Zyadatar jaane-maane products in chaaron mein se kam se kam do mein fail hote hain. Note kijiye kaun se. Aapke users aapke feature ko isi standard par parkhenge.',
  '2. Design for speed':
    '2. Speed ke liye design kijiye',
  'In Chapter 11, slow responses were a cost. Here they become a design problem. Streaming, where the answer appears piece by piece, works well for prose, because a half-arrived paragraph is already useful.':
    'Chapter 11 mein slow responses ek cost thi. Yahan woh design ki problem ban jaati hain. Streaming, jisme jawaab tukdon mein dikhta jaata hai, text ke liye achhi chalti hai, kyunki aadha aaya hua paragraph bhi kaam ka hota hai.',
  'It doesn’t work for everything. A number that appears and then changes while the answer is still being generated is worse than a spinner. The user has already read it and may have acted on it.':
    'Yeh har cheez ke liye kaam nahi karti. Ek number jo dikhta hai aur phir jawaab bante-bante badal jaata hai, woh spinner se bhi buri cheez hai. User use pehle hi padh chuka hota hai aur ho sakta hai usne uske hisaab se kuch kar bhi diya ho.',
  '3. Use confidence to choose behaviour':
    '3. Confidence se behaviour chuniye',
  'Many models can also give a confidence score along with an answer. It’s tempting to show it. Before you read on, think about what you would do with it.':
    'Bahut se models jawaab ke saath ek confidence score bhi de sakte hain. Ise dikhane ka mann karta hai. Aage padhne se pehle sochiye ki aap ise lekar kya karenge.',
  'What you would do with it':
    'Aap ise lekar kya karenge',
  'Predict: your model returns a confidence score with every answer. What should the interface do with it?':
    'Andaaza lagaiye: aapka model har jawaab ke saath ek confidence score deta hai. Interface ko ise lekar kya karna chahiye?',
  'Use it to decide what the product does, and do not display it as a number. A percentage next to an answer asks the user to judge something they cannot calibrate, and the model’s own confidence is often badly calibrated anyway. Used internally, it can change the behaviour: answer directly, show the evidence first, ask a clarifying question, or pass the case to a person.':
    'Ise yeh tay karne ke liye use kijiye ki product kya karega, aur ise number ki tarah display mat kijiye. Jawaab ke saath likha percentage user se woh cheez judge karwata hai jise woh calibrate nahi kar sakta, aur model ka apna confidence aksar vaise bhi kharab calibrated hota hai. Andar se use karne par yeh behaviour badal sakta hai: seedha jawaab dena, pehle evidence dikhana, ek clarifying sawaal poochna, ya case kisi insaan ko de dena.',
  'Turn confidence into interface choices. Use an editable field instead of a fixed one, a draft instead of a sent message, or a question instead of a guess.':
    'Confidence ko interface ke choices mein badaliye. Fixed field ki jagah editable field, bheje hue message ki jagah draft, ya andaaze ki jagah ek sawaal.',
  'Now put that into practice on a feature of your own.':
    'Ab ise apne kisi feature par karke dekhiye.',
  'Write the four states':
    'Chaar states likhiye',
  'For one feature you own, write what the user sees in four states: a confident answer, a low-confidence answer, a refusal, and the AI turned off. Write the actual screen content.':
    'Apne kisi ek feature ke liye likhiye ki user chaar states mein kya dekhta hai: ek confident jawaab, ek low-confidence jawaab, ek refusal, aur jab AI band ho. Screen par asli content likhiye.',
  'The third and fourth states are the hard ones, and most specs leave them out. If you can’t write them, the product doesn’t have them either. It just does whatever the code happens to do.':
    'Teesri aur chauthi state mushkil hain, aur zyadatar specs unhe chhod dete hain. Agar aap unhe likh nahi paate, toh product mein bhi woh nahi hain. Woh bas wahi karta hai jo code ittefaq se kar deta hai.',
  '4. Make correcting it easy':
    '4. Correction aasaan banaiye',
  'When the system is wrong, what the user does next is your most valuable data, and most products throw it away. A thumbs-down records that something was wrong, but not what.':
    'Jab system galat hota hai, tab user ka agla kadam aapka sabse keemti data hota hai, aur zyadatar products use phenk dete hain. Thumbs-down sirf yeh darj karta hai ki kuch galat tha, yeh nahi ki kya.',
  'A good correction path does three things. It captures what the user expected <em>instead</em>. It keeps the evidence the system used. And it is faster than giving up and doing the task by hand. If correcting takes longer than the manual workaround, nobody corrects, and an empty feedback table looks like satisfaction.':
    'Ek achha correction path teen kaam karta hai. Woh yeh pakadta hai ki user ne <em>uski jagah</em> kya expect kiya tha. Woh system ka use kiya hua evidence sambhaal kar rakhta hai. Aur woh haath se task kar lene se tez hota hai. Agar correct karna manual tareeke se zyada time leta hai, toh koi correct nahi karta, aur khaali feedback table satisfaction jaisa dikhta hai.',
  'Time a correction':
    'Ek correction ko time kijiye',
  'Use a clock. How long does it take to correct a wrong output, compared with doing the task by hand from scratch? Do it three times.':
    'Ghadi use kijiye. Ek galat output ko correct karne mein kitna time lagta hai, jab ise task ko shuru se haath se karne se compare karein? Teen baar kijiye.',
  'If correcting is slower, your feedback table will stay empty, and you may wrongly read that as satisfaction. This one measurement explains most of the “our users never give feedback” complaints.':
    'Agar correct karna zyada slow hai, toh aapka feedback table khaali hi rahega, aur aap ise galti se satisfaction samajh sakte hain. Yeh ek measurement “hamare users kabhi feedback nahi dete” wali zyadatar shikayaton ko samjha deti hai.',
  'Example':
    'Example',
  'Take two products that use the same model and the same retrieval. One logs a thumbs-down. The other logs the answer, the retrieved chunks and the question. It also asks one more thing: “what did you expect instead?” Within two weeks, the second has a list of real failure types and a roadmap built from its own traffic. The first has a falling number and no idea why.':
    'Do products lijiye jo same model aur same retrieval use karte hain. Ek thumbs-down log karta hai. Doosra answer, retrieved chunks aur question log karta hai, aur ek cheez aur poochta hai: “aapne uski jagah kya expect kiya tha?” Do hafte mein doosre ke paas asli failure types ki list aur apne hi traffic se bana roadmap hota hai. Pehle ke paas ek girta hua number hai aur yeh andaaza nahi ki kyun.',
  'Design the correction path for a feature you know. What does the user do when the output is wrong, what does that action record, and how long does it take compared with doing the task by hand? Then write the test case it would produce.':
    'Kisi jaane-pehchaane feature ka correction path design kijiye. Output galat hone par user kya karta hai, woh action kya record karta hai, aur haath se task karne ke comparison mein ismein kitna time lagta hai? Phir woh test case likhiye jo isse banega.',
  'the user … we capture … it takes … versus … the test case would be …':
    'user … hum pakadte hain … ismein lagta hai … jabki … test case hoga …',
  'A good design makes correcting the fastest option. Let the user edit the output in place, and treat the edit as the feedback. They were going to fix it anyway, so it costs them nothing, and you learn the expected answer exactly. Record the question, the evidence, the output and the correction together. Without the evidence, you know the answer was wrong but not whether retrieval or generation caused it, which is the distinction Chapter 19 needs.':
    'Achha design correct karne ko sabse tez option banata hai. User ko output wahin edit karne dijiye, aur edit ko hi feedback maniye. Woh use waise bhi theek karne wala tha, isliye use kuch extra nahi karna padta, aur aapko expected jawaab bilkul exact mil jaata hai. Question, evidence, output aur correction ko saath record kijiye. Evidence ke bina aapko pata hota hai ki jawaab galat tha, lekin yeh nahi ki retrieval ne gadbad ki ya generation ne, aur Chapter 19 ko yahi farq chahiye.',
  'Design the refusal':
    'Refusal design kijiye',
  'Every system looks good when it works. Trust is built on the screen that says <em>I do not have that</em>. Many AI products design that screen last, or not at all.':
    'Har system tab achha lagta hai jab woh kaam karta hai. Bharosa us screen par banta hai jo kehti hai <em>mere paas yeh nahi hai</em>. Bahut se AI products us screen ko sabse aakhir mein design karte hain, ya karte hi nahi.',
  'A system that says “I cannot answer this” and offers a route to a person earns more trust than one that always produces something. Users judge a system partly by its refusals. A feature that never refuses teaches them that its confidence means nothing.':
    'Jo system kehta hai “main iska jawaab nahi de sakta” aur kisi insaan tak pahunchne ka raasta deta hai, woh us system se zyada bharosa kamata hai jo hamesha kuch na kuch bana deta hai. Users system ko kuch had tak uske refusals se judge karte hain. Jo feature kabhi refuse nahi karta, woh unhe sikhaata hai ki uske confidence ka koi matlab nahi.',
  'What happens when the AI is off':
    'Jab AI band ho toh kya hota hai',
  'Finally, decide what the feature does when the AI is switched off. In Chapter 18 you saw that a rollback and a kill switch are different things.':
    'Aakhir mein tay kijiye ki jab AI switch off ho toh feature kya karega. Chapter 18 mein aapne dekha tha ki rollback aur kill switch alag cheezein hain.',
  'A kill switch is only usable if there is something underneath it. That could be search without generated answers, a form without extraction, or a queue without routing. If turning the AI off leaves a blank screen, the switch just replaces one failure with another.':
    'Kill switch tabhi kaam ka hai jab uske neeche kuch ho. Jaise generated jawaab ke bina search, extraction ke bina form, ya routing ke bina queue. Agar AI band karne par screen khaali ho jaati hai, toh switch bas ek failure ki jagah doosri failure rakh deta hai.',
  'To sum up, a feature earns trust by showing its evidence, staying quick, using confidence to choose what it does, and making corrections easy. Add a refusal that leads to a person, and a fallback for when the AI is off. Next, you’ll learn how to find out whether all this actually helped the business, by designing a pilot.':
    'Toh chhote mein, ek feature bharosa tab kamata hai jab woh apna evidence dikhaye, tez rahe, confidence se tay kare ki kya karna hai, aur correction aasaan banaye. Saath mein ek aisi refusal jo insaan tak le jaaye, aur AI band hone par ek fallback. Aage aap seekhenge ki yeh sab business ke liye asal mein kaam aaya ya nahi, yeh kaise pata kiya jaaye, ek pilot design karke.',
  'Design the four answers the tool can give':
    'Woh chaar jawaab design kijiye jo tool de sakta hai',
  'Many AI features use the same screen when they are sure, when they are unsure, when they are wrong and when they cannot answer. So a confident wrong answer looks exactly like a confident right one. In this chapter you learned how a feature that is wrong one time in ten can still be trusted, and why a confidence number is usually the wrong thing to show. Now you’ll design the four states for the developer kit.':
    'Bahut se AI features ek hi screen use karte hain, chahe woh sure ho, unsure ho, galat ho ya jawaab na de sake. Isliye ek confident galat jawaab bilkul ek confident sahi jawaab jaisa dikhta hai. Is chapter mein aapne seekha ki jo feature 10 mein se ek baar galat hota hai, us par bhi bharosa kaise kiya ja sakta hai, aur confidence number dikhana aam taur par galat kyun hai. Ab aap developer kit ke liye chaar states design karenge.',
  'This task is written, with no code. Design on paper, time one correction with a stopwatch, and write your design in the notes box below.':
    'Yeh task likhne ka hai, code nahi. Paper par design kijiye, ek correction ko stopwatch se time kijiye, aur apna design neeche wale notes box mein likhiye.',
  'Look at an AI feature you use every day. Note what it shows when it is confident, when it is unsure, when it is wrong and when it cannot answer.':
    'Ek AI feature dekhiye jo aap roz use karte hain. Note kijiye ki woh confident hone par, unsure hone par, galat hone par aur jawaab na de paane par kya dikhata hai.',
  'Now design the four states for Bharat Privacy Guard: found and removed with confidence, found but unsure, wrongly removed (a false alarm), and unable to decide. For each state, write what the developer’s program receives, what the end user sees, and what each of them can do next.':
    'Ab Bharat Privacy Guard ke liye chaar states design kijiye: confidence ke saath mila aur hata diya gaya, mila lekin unsure, galti se hata diya gaya (false alarm), aur tay nahi kar paaya. Har state ke liye likhiye ki developer ke program ko kya milta hai, end user ko kya dikhta hai, aur dono aage kya kar sakte hain.',
  'Design the “unable to decide” state as a real way to reach a person, not a dead end. Say who the person is and how long they take.':
    '“Tay nahi kar paaya” state ko kisi asli insaan tak pahunchne ka raasta banaiye, band gali nahi. Batayiye ki woh insaan kaun hai aur use kitna time lagta hai.',
  'Decide what the tool shows when it is only half sure, so that the uncertainty is visible without being noisy. Don’t show a bare confidence number.':
    'Tay kijiye ki jab tool sirf aadha sure ho toh woh kya dikhaye, taaki uncertainty dikhe lekin shor na ho. Sirf ek confidence number mat dikhaiye.',
  'Time one correction from start to finish. Start when a user notices that a harmless word was hidden, and stop when the rule is fixed. Count every step and every hand-off.':
    'Ek correction ko shuru se aakhir tak time kijiye. Tab shuru kijiye jab user dekhta hai ki ek harmless word chhupa diya gaya, aur tab roki jab rule theek ho jaaye. Har step aur har hand-off gin lijiye.',
  'Write down which of the four states your current design merges together, and what that costs the user.':
    'Likhiye ki aapka maujooda design chaar states mein se kaun se ko ek mein mila deta hai, aur user ko isse kya nuksaan hota hai.',
  'All four states written out, including what each person can do in each one.':
    'Chaaron states likhi hui, jisme har state mein har insaan kya kar sakta hai woh bhi ho.',
  'The “unable to decide” route to a real person.':
    '“Tay nahi kar paaya” ka kisi asli insaan tak jaane wala raasta.',
  'The correction path, timed from start to finish, with the slowest step marked.':
    'Correction path, shuru se aakhir tak time kiya hua, sabse slow step par nishaan ke saath.',
  'The state your product handles worst, and the first thing you would change.':
    'Woh state jo aapka product sabse kharab handle karta hai, aur sabse pehli cheez jo aap badlenge.',

  /* ---- ch205: Measuring business impact: designing a pilot ---- */
  'Measuring business impact: designing a pilot':
    'Business impact naapna: ek pilot design karna',
  'A system can be accurate and still change nothing for the business. In this chapter you’ll find the slow part of a workflow, then design a pilot with a business metric, a comparison, a failure threshold and a time window.':
    'Ek system accurate ho sakta hai, phir bhi business ke liye kuch nahi badalta. Is chapter mein aap ek workflow ka slow hissa dhoondhenge, phir ek pilot design karenge jisme business metric, comparison, failure threshold aur time window hoga.',
  'You can measure the system':
    'Aap system ko measure kar sakte hain',
  'That means recall, precision, judges and per-category scores.':
    'Matlab recall, precision, judges aur per-category scores.',
  'The interface has four states':
    'Interface ki chaar states hain',
  'What the user sees decides whether quality reaches them.':
    'User ko jo dikhta hai, wahi tay karta hai ki quality us tak pahunchti hai ya nahi.',
  'Design a pilot that could actually show the feature did not help.':
    'Aisa pilot design kar paana jo asal mein dikha sake ki feature se madad nahi mili.',
  'Name the one number the business cares about, and how the feature would move it.':
    'Woh ek number bata paana jo business ke liye matter karta hai, aur feature use kaise hilayega.',
  'Explain why a system that measures well can still produce no business benefit.':
    'Samjha paana ki jo system achhe se measure hota hai, woh bhi business ko koi fayda kyun nahi de sakta.',
  'You don’t need any code for this chapter. Bring a feature you have measured, yours or one from the course, and the name of whoever pays for it.':
    'Is chapter ke liye code nahi chahiye. Ek aisa feature saath rakhiye jise aapne measure kiya hai, aapka ya course ka, aur us insaan ka naam jo uske liye paisa deta hai.',
  'So far the course has measured the system itself. Is retrieval right? Can the judge be trusted? Is the classifier accurate in each category? All of that is necessary.':
    'Ab tak course ne system ko hi measure kiya hai. Kya retrieval sahi hai? Kya judge par bharosa kiya ja sakta hai? Kya classifier har category mein accurate hai? Yeh sab zaroori hai.',
  'But none of it answers the question that decides future funding: did it help the business? That is what this chapter is about, and it ends with a pilot you can actually design.':
    'Lekin inme se koi bhi us sawaal ka jawaab nahi deta jo aage ki funding tay karta hai: kya isse business ko madad mili? Yeh chapter isi ke baare mein hai, aur aakhir tak aap ek pilot design kar paayenge.',
  'A system can be 94% accurate and change nothing, because nobody uses it, or because the step it speeds up was never the slow part.':
    'Ek system 94% accurate ho sakta hai aur phir bhi kuch nahi badalta, kyunki ya toh koi use nahi karta, ya jis step ko woh tez karta hai woh kabhi slow hissa tha hi nahi.',
  'Find the slow part first':
    'Pehle slow hissa dhoondhiye',
  'Before you design anything, check that you are speeding up the right step. The way to do that is to time the whole workflow.':
    'Kuch bhi design karne se pehle check kijiye ki aap sahi step ko tez kar rahe hain ya nahi. Iska tareeka hai poore workflow ko time karna.',
  'Time the whole workflow':
    'Poore workflow ko time kijiye',
  'Take the workflow your feature is part of and time it honestly from start to finish. Include every step, including the time spent waiting for other people.':
    'Woh workflow lijiye jiska aapka feature hissa hai aur use shuru se aakhir tak imaandaari se time kijiye. Har step shaamil kijiye, doosre logon ka intezaar karne ka time bhi.',
  'The AI step is rarely the largest. Often the biggest block is waiting for a person, or a handover between two systems. If you turn a four-minute step into a one-minute step inside a three-day process, nobody will notice.':
    'AI wala step shaayad hi kabhi sabse bada hota hai. Aksar sabse bada hissa kisi insaan ka intezaar hota hai, ya do systems ke beech ka handover. Agar aap teen din ke process ke andar chaar minute ke step ko ek minute ka kar dein, toh kisi ko pata bhi nahi chalega.',
  'This is the cheapest study in the course, and the one most often skipped, because it can show that you should not build the feature.':
    'Yeh course ki sabse sasti study hai, aur sabse zyada chhodi jaane wali, kyunki yeh dikha sakti hai ki aapko woh feature banana hi nahi chahiye.',
  'Four parts of a pilot':
    'Pilot ke chaar hisse',
  'Suppose the step you are speeding up really is on the critical path. Then you can design a pilot. Think of a pilot as a test that states in advance what result would disappoint you, not as a small launch.':
    'Maan lijiye jis step ko aap tez kar rahe hain, woh sach mein critical path par hai. Tab aap pilot design kar sakte hain. Pilot ko ek chhote launch ki tarah nahi, balki ek aise test ki tarah dekhiye jo pehle se bata deta hai ki kaunsa result aapko nirash karega.',
  'A pilot has four parts. Here they are in order.':
    'Pilot ke chaar hisse hote hain. Yeh order mein hain.',
  '<strong>The number.</strong> Choose one measure the business already tracks, such as handling time, first-contact resolution, backlog age or error rate. Don’t invent a new metric for the pilot.':
    '<strong>Number.</strong> Ek aisa measure chuniye jo business pehle se track karta hai, jaise handling time, first-contact resolution, backlog age ya error rate. Pilot ke liye koi naya metric mat banaiye.',
  '<strong>The comparison.</strong> Looking at the same team before and after is weak evidence. Comparing two similar groups over the same period is much stronger, and it is usually possible.':
    '<strong>Comparison.</strong> Ek hi team ko pehle aur baad mein dekhna kamzor evidence hai. Ek hi period mein do milte-julte groups ko compare karna kaafi mazboot hai, aur aam taur par yeh mumkin hota hai.',
  '<strong>The failure threshold.</strong> This is the change below which you will call the pilot a failure. Write it down before you start.':
    '<strong>Failure threshold.</strong> Yeh woh badlaav hai jisse neeche aap pilot ko failure kahenge. Ise shuru karne se pehle likh lijiye.',
  '<strong>The time window.</strong> Make it long enough for the novelty to wear off. Any new tool looks better than it really is in its first two weeks.':
    '<strong>Time window.</strong> Ise itna lamba rakhiye ki novelty khatam ho jaaye. Koi bhi naya tool apne pehle do hafton mein asli se behtar dikhta hai.',
  'The failure threshold is the part people skip. So let’s practise it.':
    'Failure threshold wahi hissa hai jo log chhod dete hain. Chaliye isse practise karte hain.',
  'Write the disappointing result in advance':
    'Pehle se likhiye ki nirash karne wala result kya hoga',
  'Before running anything, write the sentence you would have to say if the pilot showed no effect. Use the words you would say to the person who funded it.':
    'Kuch bhi chalane se pehle woh sentence likhiye jo aapko kehna padega agar pilot ne koi asar nahi dikhaya. Wahi shabd likhiye jo aap us insaan se kahenge jisne ise fund kiya.',
  'This shows you two things. First, whether you really have a failure threshold, because many pilots don’t. Second, whether the pilot could ever produce that sentence, or whether every outcome would be reported as a success.':
    'Isse aapko do cheezein dikhti hain. Pehli, kya aapke paas sach mein failure threshold hai, kyunki bahut se pilots mein nahi hota. Doosri, kya yeh pilot kabhi woh sentence paida kar sakta hai, ya har result ko success hi bataya jaayega.',
  'If a pilot cannot fail, it is really a launch.':
    'Agar pilot fail nahi ho sakta, toh woh asal mein ek launch hai.',
  'When the system works but the number does not move':
    'Jab system kaam kare lekin number na hile',
  'Sometimes you run a careful pilot and the system works, yet the business number stays where it was. Where do you look first?':
    'Kabhi-kabhi aap ek dhyaan se kiya hua pilot chalate hain, system kaam karta hai, phir bhi business number wahin ka wahin rehta hai. Aap sabse pehle kahan dekhenge?',
  'Your pilot shows the system works well, but the business number does not move. Write the three explanations you would investigate first, in order.':
    'Aapka pilot dikhata hai ki system achhe se kaam karta hai, lekin business number nahi hilta. Woh teen wajah likhiye jinki aap sabse pehle jaanch karenge, order mein.',
  '1 … 2 … 3 …':
    '1 … 2 … 3 …',
  'This order usually works best. First, are people using it? Low adoption is the most common cause and the easiest to check. Second, does the output reach the place where the decision is made, or is it in a separate tool someone has to remember to open? Third, was the step you sped up actually on the critical path? Only after these three should you question the quality of the system. Teams usually check quality first because they know how to measure it.':
    'Yeh order aam taur par sabse achha chalta hai. Pehla, kya log ise use kar rahe hain? Kam adoption sabse common wajah hai aur check karna sabse aasaan. Doosra, kya output wahan pahunchta hai jahan decision liya jaata hai, ya woh ek alag tool mein hai jise kholna kisi ko yaad rakhna padta hai? Teesra, kya jis step ko aapne tez kiya woh asal mein critical path par tha? In teen ke baad hi system ki quality par sawaal uthaiye. Teams aksar quality pehle check karti hain kyunki unhe use measure karna aata hai.',
  'The whole course repeats one idea at a larger and larger scale. Decide what counts as failure, then measure that specific thing, instead of looking at outputs and forming an impression. You have done this for a prompt, a retriever, a judge, a cost model and now a business case.':
    'Poora course ek hi idea ko badi aur badi scale par dohraata hai. Tay kijiye ki failure kise kehte hain, phir usi khaas cheez ko measure kijiye, outputs dekh kar ek impression banane ke bajaye. Aap yeh ek prompt, ek retriever, ek judge, ek cost model aur ab ek business case ke liye kar chuke hain.',
  'The techniques will change as models change. The habit of defining failure first and then measuring it is what carries over.':
    'Techniques models ke saath badalti rahengi. Pehle failure define karne aur phir use measure karne ki aadat hi aage tak saath chalti hai.',
  'Next, you’ll move to the building blocks you need to work with AI systems yourself: Python, HTTP, Git and tests.':
    'Aage aap un building blocks par jaayenge jo AI systems ke saath khud kaam karne ke liye chahiye: Python, HTTP, Git aur tests.',
  'Design a pilot that could fail':
    'Aisa pilot design kijiye jo fail ho sakta hai',
  'A pilot that cannot fail proves nothing. In this chapter you learned to pick one number the business already tracks, to compare two groups, and to agree a failure threshold before the pilot starts. Now you’ll design a pilot of the developer kit with five to ten real developers.':
    'Jo pilot fail nahi ho sakta, woh kuch saabit nahi karta. Is chapter mein aapne seekha ki ek aisa number chunna jo business pehle se track karta hai, do groups ko compare karna, aur pilot shuru hone se pehle failure threshold par agreement banana. Ab aap developer kit ka ek pilot design karenge, paanch se das asli developers ke saath.',
  'This task is written, with no code. Design on paper, then write it up and get it agreed before anything runs.':
    'Yeh task likhne ka hai, code nahi. Paper par design kijiye, phir likh kar use agree karwaiye, kuch bhi chalne se pehle.',
  'Time the whole workflow of a developer who handles user text, and mark where the kit fits. If it is not on the critical path, say so and stop here.':
    'Ek developer ka poora workflow time kijiye jo user text handle karta hai, aur mark kijiye ki kit kahan fit hota hai. Agar woh critical path par nahi hai, toh yeh kahiye aur yahin ruk jaaiye.',
  'Choose the business number. It must already exist and someone must already look at it, for example the number of support tickets that mention personal data being pasted into a chat.':
    'Business number chuniye. Woh pehle se maujood hona chahiye aur koi use pehle se dekhta ho, jaise un support tickets ka number jo chat mein personal data paste hone ka zikr karte hain.',
  'Design the comparison. Say which two groups of developers or which two periods you will compare, and why they are comparable.':
    'Comparison design kijiye. Batayiye ki developers ke kaun se do groups ya kaun se do periods ko aap compare karenge, aur woh comparable kyun hain.',
  'Write the failure threshold, which is the change below which you will call the pilot a failure. Also write the time window you will wait before you judge.':
    'Failure threshold likhiye, yaani woh badlaav jisse neeche aap pilot ko failure kahenge. Saath mein woh time window bhi likhiye jiska aap judge karne se pehle intezaar karenge.',
  'Write the disappointing result in advance, in the words you would say to whoever funded it.':
    'Pehle se nirash karne wala result likhiye, un shabdon mein jo aap ise fund karne wale se kahenge.',
  'Get the person who funds it to agree the threshold in writing before the pilot runs.':
    'Jo insaan ise fund karta hai, pilot chalne se pehle usse threshold written mein agree karwaiye.',
  'A business number that was already being tracked before the pilot.':
    'Ek business number jo pilot se pehle se track ho raha tha.',
  'A comparison between two groups or periods, with a reason they are fair.':
    'Do groups ya periods ke beech ek comparison, jisme yeh wajah ho ki woh fair kyun hain.',
  'A failure threshold agreed in writing by the person who would be disappointed by it.':
    'Ek failure threshold jo us insaan ne written mein agree kiya ho jise woh nirash karega.',
  'A description of a result that would make you recommend switching the kit off.':
    'Ek aise result ka description jo aapko kit ko switch off karne ki recommendation dene par majboor kare.'
});
